\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Piotr Dunhilton, CEO of the Airborne Meritocracy of Ulyssia, sent a Gerald Hardman a brief, Commissioner of the Department of Traffic Movement and Property Security, a single brief:\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f2\i\fs22 With all due urgency, using any resources necessary, recover a samples of the Lentil Virus.
\f1\i0 \
Gerry generously assumed Dunhilton was using code to refer to the \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 lentiviral vector\cf2 \outl0\strokewidth0  that 
\f2\i HIBReLabs 
\f1\i0 called TMinusVirus. A minute later, he received a text message from Dunhilton. In summary: if he failed, every financial detail of his would be delivered to the High Tea Lady of the Ulyssian Tax Office. Dunhilton would see him bankrupt before he sent him on extended holiday.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Gerry set his considerable team in motion. They began with what everyone ranked as the lowest-risk prospect: a small facility dug into the Western Desert\'92s forgiving sedimentary rock, perfect for beta-testing their excavation techniques. Out here, any disturbance was fair game\'97noise, dust, loose earth, even a spontaneous geyser could erupt and nobody credible would complain. They could risk a full-blown zombie apocalypse of long-lost scientists and still keep it contained.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 The Western Desert site delivered no virus, but it did yield a tax-you addendum: tucked away in an office was documentation on another dozen HIBReLabs. Gerry tossed caution to the rock saws and diamond drills and ordered all geographically easy sites cracked open three at a time. As the project grew, he reviewed the army of equipment with a secret flush of pride; this was serious resource command, serious budget.\
But as the weeks wore on, dread gnawed through Gerry\'92s optimism. Every bit of experience screamed that the true virus site would be the very one he least wanted breached: a subterranean bunker under the most expensive real estate in Ulyssia, among the Airborne towers, their tentacular pylons plunging through the Layer for anchorage in the city\'92s inner core, the district called the Pale. The Pale was infamous: poorest neighbourhood, Layer thick as gravy, weather always set to \'93toxic.\'94 Causing any explosions under those towers was a recipe for public relations collapse, if not whole-building catastrophe. For now, Gerry played it safe, delaying the worst-case dig while working through easier targets he called, in the reports, \'93methodology tests\'94.\
Not that those were a walk in the park. A clear pattern appeared: every lab was sunk at least fifty metres down, under solid strata\'97mostly uncut volcanic rock or dense conglomerate\'97and reinforced with ultra-high-performance concrete ten metres thick. All the lift wells were plugged with more concrete; Jamison built the labs and then made sure they were practically inaccessible once he had decommissioned them.\
Labour was slow and exacting. The deeper they bored, the more delicate the process; if they triggered a cave-in, the archives and offices below might be lost, along with any clues to the virus\'92s real resting place.\
Over weeks, they opened and combed through nearly all the labs. Each successful breach brought fresh evidence of yet more sites: a scavenger hunt with more steps at every turn. Gerry threw more resources at the problem until only the one under the Pale remained, known only from cryptic financial paperwork, always surfacing in files mislaid by other facility accountants. That it might house HIBReLabs work was borderline unthinkable.\
No more stalling. It was time for Gerry to reckon with the Pale site\'97likely home to the TMinusVirus, and, if worst came to worst, to living, decades-old nerd fossils.
